A||N||2||XDrewX||Tips for replacing belts on v6 with air||||5||
Z||000000||XDrewX||06-17-2001||03:57 AM||||Being that any work on the engine is like trying to build a ship in a bottle, I was just looking for any tips on replacing the belts on a 87 v6 with air.  I was thinking of removing the battery to gain more access for starters.<P>Any tricks, opinions, stories are welcome.<P>Thanx||||reg||5
Z||000001||87GTZ34||06-17-2001||11:38 AM||||Drew,  You have the wrong approach to fixing Fieros...  granted they are different than 'normal' cars but not too bad to work on.  The main difference is that front mounted engine GM mobiles have several ways to attack whereas the Fiero has very limited ways to work on them but once you get used to thinking differently its not so bad.  For the belts remove the vent grate, battery as you suggested, jack up the right rear wheel and take the wheel off.  You now will have access to bolts you need to change the belts. GL||||reg||1
Z||000002||XDrewX||06-17-2001||11:48 AM||||thanks, i didn't think about taking off the wheel.<P>I didn't mean to make a bad comment about the fiero, it is not hard to work on at all if you know the tricks, unfourtanetly, I have short arms (at least to me they seem short).  <P>for example on my 99 grand prix, I could not for the life of me figure out how to get the spark plugs from the back of the engine.  Then i found out you remove the bolt in the motor mount and rock the engine forward and PRESTO, no big deal to get to the plugs.<P>I have only had my fiero for about 3 weeks, so i have alot to learn. ||||reg||1
